  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "open_connection",
      "components": ["U1", "U2", "U3", "U4"],
      "pins": ["U1.20", "U1.21", "U1.22", "U2.5", "U3.5", "U4.5"],
      "nets": ["SCK", "CS1", "CS2", "GPIO05", "GPIO06", "GPIO07"],
      "observed": "On the mcu sheet the global labels attached to U1 are CS0 on GPIO04 (pin 19), CS1 on GPIO05 (pin 20), CS2 on GPIO06 (pin 21) and SO on GPIO10 (pin 16); GPIO07 (pin 22) is left unconnected and there is NO SCK label anywhere on U1. The thermocouple sheet still carries a global label SCK on U2/U3/U4 pin 5, so the SCK net has no driver.",
      "expected": "The spec states SCK is tied to the ESP32 on GPIO5 and the chip-selects CS0/CS1/CS2 are driven by GPIO4/GPIO6/GPIO7. Here GPIO5 is wrongly assigned CS1, GPIO6 wrongly CS2, GPIO7 (the intended CS2) is open, and the SPI clock line SCK is not connected to the MCU at all.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "mcu.kicad_sch, U1 right column: 'GPIO04/ADC1_CH4 19 -> CS0', 'GPIO05/ADC2_CH0 20 -> CS1', 'GPIO06 21 -> CS2', 'GPIO07 22 -> (no label)', 'GPIO10 16 -> SO'; no SCK label on U1"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "thermocouples.kicad_sch: 'SCK' global label on U2 pin 5, U3 pin 5, U4 pin 5"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "SCK (pin 5) and SO (pin 7 ...) are common nets (SCK, SO) tied to all three ICs and to the ESP32 (U1 GPIO5/SCK, GPIO10/SO). Each chip has its own independent chip-select ... CS0/CS1/CS2, driven by ESP32 GPIO4/GPIO6/GPIO7 respectively."}
      ],
      "impact": "The SPI clock to all three MAX31855 thermocouple interfaces is undriven, so no temperature can be read; the chip-selects are also on the wrong GPIOs (CS1/CS2 on GPIO5/GPIO6, GPIO7 unused), which would corrupt selection even if SCK were restored. The oven controller loses all thermocouple sensing.",
      "confidence": 0.85
    },
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["C4"],
      "pins": ["C4.1", "C4.2"],
      "nets": ["+3V3", "GND"],
      "observed": "On the thermocouple sheet the decoupling capacitor C4 (placed at U3) is labelled 1mF, while the identical decoupling caps C3 and C6 are 1uF.",
      "expected": "The spec calls for a 1 uF decoupling cap per thermocouple channel; a 1 mF (1000 uF) value in a small SOIC-8 decoupling position is three orders of magnitude too large and physically implausible for the footprint.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "thermocouples.kicad_sch: 'C3 1uF', 'C4 1mF', 'C6 1uF'"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "a 1 µF decoupling cap per channel (C3 for U2, C6 for U3, C4 for U4 ...)"}
      ],
      "impact": "A 1 mF cap on the 3V3 rail of a MAX31855 would have huge ESR/leakage and an inrush/charge profile inconsistent with the intended 1 uF decoupling, degrading or destabilising that thermocouple channel's supply filtering.",
      "confidence": 0.9
    },
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["J12", "U5"],
      "pins": ["J12.A4", "J12.A9", "U5.1", "U5.2"],
      "nets": ["VBUS", "+5V"],
      "observed": "On the mcu sheet the USB-C receptacle J12 VBUS power pins carry the global label 'VBUS' (and are also tied to a +5V power arrow), while on the power sheet U5 (TPS2121) IN2 = +5V and OUT = VBUS. The 'VBUS' label therefore joins the mux output rail directly to the USB 5V input net.",
      "expected": "The spec says the USB VBUS pins form the +5V net feeding IN2, and the mux OUT forms a separate internal VBUS rail that is distinct from the USB +5V upstream of the mux. The two rails must not be merged.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "mcu.kicad_sch, J12: 'A4 VBUS' global label on the USB-C VBUS pin, with '+5V' arrow on the same connector; power.kicad_sch, U5: 'IN2 +5V', 'OUT VBUS'"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "J12 ... VBUS pins (A4/A9/B4/B9) -> +5V net -> U5 pin 2/2_1 (IN2) ... outputs a single OUT ... onto the VBUS net (the board's internal, unregulated/pre-buck rail — note this net is named VBUS in the netlist but is distinct from the USB +5V net upstream of the mux)."}
      ],
      "impact": "Shorting the mux output (VBUS) to the USB input (+5V/IN2) defeats the power-mux arbitration: with the 12 V barrel jack present the mux would back-drive the USB VBUS/+5V net toward the host, and the seamless switchover is lost.",
      "confidence": 0.6
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The build deviates from the reference design in at least two clear ways: the SPI clock (SCK) is never connected to the ESP32 and the chip-selects are mis-mapped onto GPIO5/GPIO6 (leaving GPIO7 open), which would break all thermocouple reading; and decoupling cap C4 is 1 mF instead of the specified 1 uF. A third, lower-confidence issue is that the USB-C VBUS pins are labelled 'VBUS', merging the mux output rail with the +5V input and defeating the power mux."
}

